or column-constraint checks. T10-R6 makes the duplicate and unique-overlap pairings tri-state — an otherwise-applicable pair blocked by a withheld `IndexKindUnknown` candidate reports the bounded `target_table.indexes` gap instead of silently passing (kind truth lives on the member, not its key parts), while fully comparable pairs and proven members answer normally (issue #89 T10-R6). T11-R2 extends the shared ordered-column premise: `statementColumnDecls` folds same-statement column actions in authored order (MySQL evaluates the key action against the effective statement-local declaration — an `ADD` supplies a member the pre-state never had, a `MODIFY` supplies the new definition, a `CHANGE`/`RENAME` carries the prior name's proven facts under the new spelling — the pass-through returns a copy rebound to the new member name so findings attribute the effective column, never the pre-state's old spelling, and the provider object is never mutated; TiDB validates every action against the pre-state so any touched member is undecidable — both probed natively), and `orderedColumnFact` returns a tri-state verdict — resolved / proven-absent (the native refusal, no gap) / undecidable (the bounded `target_table.columns` gap) — shared verbatim by Evaluate and EvidenceGaps (issue #90 T11-R2 group A) |
