tered` for any member shape (the TiDB DROP PRIMARY KEY 8200 refusal covers PKIsHandle and common-handle clustered primaries alike) while `CLUSTERED` plus the single-integer member shape separately resolves `PrimaryKeyIsHandle` (STATISTICS synthesizes a PRIMARY row even for handle tables, so index presence alone cannot prove an operable PRIMARY object — a missing CLUSTERED row sets both `PrimaryKeyClusteredUnknown` and `PrimaryKeyHandleUnknown`, and an unmatchable member shape sets `PrimaryKeyHandleUnknown`, rather than defaulting the object into existence), and the TiDB instance-fact probe additionally reads the `tidb_enable_clustered_index` session variable so declared-but-unstated primary keys resolve through the observed mode (empty means unprobed → the handle answer stays unknown; never populated on MySQL). R2 fixes member classification for TiDB's literal-`'NULL'` `COLUMN_NAME` on expression members: SQL NULL or literal-with-expression-text stays an expression member, a literal matching a loaded real column name stays a plain column (a backticked `` `NULL` `` column is legal MySQL), and neither-provable stays `UnmodeledKeyParts` (issue #89 T10-R2). R4 adds `resolveTiDBPrimaryName`; R5 rebinds it to the actual stored object name: a `primary`-fold catalog row is not self-evidently the constraint object on TiDB (a secondary can literally own `primary` — `RENAME … TO primary` is legal on PK-less and PKIsHandle tables — and a renamed PK object survives under `pk2`/`pk3`), so constraint presence is proven independently by the `information_schema.table_constraints` `PRIMARY KEY` row (tidb_pk_type cannot answer it — NONCLUSTERED covers both PK-less tables and real nonclustered primaries, verified on the TiDB v8.5.0 anchor) while object attribution is proven separately: a proven constraint with exactly one unique candidate fold promotes it to `snapshot.PrimaryKey` under its stored name, multiple candidates disambiguate through `TIDB_INDEXES.CLUSTERED` (exactly one clust